# The `kvm` variant (scripts/build-kernel.sh ARCH OUT kvm): KVM, for a guest that is
# itself a KVM host, on arm64 booted at EL2 (`run --kernel --nested`, D132); and VFIO, for
# a guest to take a PCI function it was given in its own userspace, its interrupts among
# it (D154). Not in the kernel every other guest boots: KVM's 800 KiB of text would be in
# each, for no boot or restore time saved or spent (PM M192).
CONFIG_VIRTUALIZATION=y
CONFIG_KVM=y
# VFIO, for such a guest to pass a PCI device it was given to a guest of its own, as a
# host passes one of its own (D154): vfio-pci takes the device, and with no IOMMU in the
# guest its group is a no-IOMMU one (Documentation/driver-api/vfio.rst), fit for devices
# that make no DMA, as the test's (QEMU's edu, D153).
CONFIG_VFIO=y
CONFIG_VFIO_PCI=y
CONFIG_VFIO_NOIOMMU=y
# virtio-iommu (drivers/iommu/virtio-iommu.c), the IOMMU the VMM gives the functions it
# emulates (D154): with it, such a function is in a group with an IOMMU, and its DMA,
# mapped through VFIO's type 1, reaches only what the guest mapped; ACPI's VIOT
# describes it on x86_64, the devicetree on arm64.
CONFIG_VIRTIO_IOMMU=y
CONFIG_ACPI_VIOT=y
# iommufd and VFIO's device nodes (/dev/iommu, /dev/vfio/devices/vfioN), the other way a
# process takes a PCI function through VFIO: a device by a node of its own, its DMA in an
# I/O address space of iommufd's (Documentation/userspace-api/iommufd.rst), as hosts that
# give a user a device but not its group do (D154). The group and its container stay.
CONFIG_IOMMUFD=y
CONFIG_VFIO_DEVICE_CDEV=y
